what if everything in the universe was  actually a bit to the right of where it  is now or if this orbiting planet was  actually half a rotation ahead  what changes more importantly what stays  the same  these seem like fun but useless thought  experiments until Emmy Noether  discovered what I think is the most  profound and far-reaching idea in  physics knowing what happens to a system  under these imaginary transformations  gives us insight into the system's real  behavior  the usual summary is symmetries imply  conservation laws in this video I'll  explain what that means  we'll start with symmetry normally we  use the word symmetry to mean that if we  took the mirror image along some line a  symmetric object looks the same mirror  symmetries like these are pretty but we  can make the word symmetry means so much  more  for example rotational symmetry when you  can rotate an object by a certain amount  and it looks just the same as before or  another example is translational  symmetry in fact mathematicians took the  idea of symmetry and generalized it  completely a symmetry is anything where  you have some sort of object and apply  some sort of transformation to it and  you can't tell the difference in some  sense this might seem like they've taken  a good descriptive word and then  generalized it until it's meaningless  but actually this idea is very useful  these abstract symmetries are a  constantly reoccurring theme in  mathematics in fact the study of  symmetry helped motivate one of the most  important fields of modern mathematics  called abstract algebra Emmy Noether was  an expert in symmetry having developed  foundational concepts in abstract  algebra it was during a small pause from  her extremely influential mathematical  career that she thought about physics  she wondered if she could apply the idea  of symmetry to the world and that's what  led her to her beautiful theorem this is  the symmetry that she considered the  object is some system a part of the  universe it could be a thing someone's  throwing or a particle in a void or  maybe some binary stars or if you want  the whole universe then you transform it  for example you could rotate it by some  angle lambda or shift it up or down by  lambda or stretch all the distances by  lambda now we're interested in if this  system is the same in some sense northa  decided the interesting thing to check  is if the total energy of the objects  would be the same so we say that a  system has a symmetry under a  transformation if the total energy of  the objects didn't change after that  transformation for example if I had a  particle all by itself and then compared  it to a shifted version clearly the  energies are the same so this system is  translationally symmetric on the other  hand say there was some big planet  nearby a particle that is closer has got  more gravitational potential energy and  so this isn't translationally symmetric  or consider this object orbiting in a  circle and compare it to a rotated  version both objects are an equal  distance from the planet and so both  ways they have the same gravitational  potential energy and so this system is  rotationally symmetric so that's the  symmetry part of know--this theorem now  let's look at conservation x'  if you've ever studied physics for  example at school you'll know how  important these things called  conservation laws are it means that if  you have a bunch of things and you count  it up their total momentum let's say  then you let them go for any amount of  time and countered the momentum again it  would be the same number  technically you can do physics without  ever needing to use these conservation  laws but sometimes you'll be given some  insane problem that looks like you  shouldn't be able to solve at least not  very easily but if you invoke the  magical conservation laws your answer  just falls out conservation laws aren't  just useful in classical physics either  they help out in quantum mechanics and  really all of modern physics I used to  not like using conservation laws because  they can make it seem too easy as in I  get this  solution with so little work that it  really felt like magic and so I didn't  feel like I understood why it worked  after all I didn't understand why energy  is conserved or why momentum is  conserved and so if I used one of those  to solve a problem then clearly I didn't  understand the solution note this  theorem is powerful because it tries to  explain where conservation come from so  let me go back to an example I said that  momentum is conserved but this is kind  of not always true if I trace my system  to be a ball rolling on the ground we  all know that eventually it stops or if  I drop something it gets faster and  faster sure if you take everything as  your system then momentum is always  conserved but how can I know whether a  particular systems mentum won't change  no --this theorem gives us a simple way  to know regardless of whether the system  is one particle or the whole universe  she proves that you only get  conservation x' if the system had the  right symmetries again let's look at  examples if you have translational  symmetry the theorem says that you have  conservation of momentum we know that a  particle that's on its own has this  symmetry and so its momentum is  conserved and we know that's true it  will continue on at the same speed in  the same direction forever if we instead  had a bunch of particles by themselves  as our system this system is also  translationally symmetric if they were  all over there instead that doesn't  change their energy so again Nothe tells  us that we have conservation of their  total momentum which wouldn't be that  obvious otherwise in fact if we  considered a shifted over version of the  entire universe no one would be able to  tell the difference and so there's  clearly no difference in the energy  hence the momentum of the whole universe  is conserved when isn't momentum  conserved for a system what about this  object that gains speed as it falls note  this theorem says that this system can't  have translational symmetry so let's  check what if this object was nearer to  the ground  it would have less gravitational  potential energy good  so it isn't symmetric what about  rotational symmetry like we said this  object could have been rotated here and  the energy wouldn't change and so it has  rotational symmetry about this axis we  also know it has angular momentum in  this direction and that it goes at the  same speed the whole way around and so  it's angular momentum is constant in  other words conserved and this is what  know--this Theron predicts that if you  have rotational symmetry about one axis  then the angular momentum in that  direction is conserved so one last  example and this one is a weird one  we've talked about translating in space  and in angle but what about translating  in time in other words you have a system  doing something at a particular moment  and then you compare it to the same  system sometime later if it has the same  energy then it is time translation  symmetric  what does Nothe say is conserved then  it's the energy I know that's a bit  circular here but it's more important  when we come to quantum mechanics so I  had to mention this one Noether didn't  just come up with these three examples  instead she gave us a mathematical way  to turn any symmetry into a conservation  and vice-versa  see these conserved quantities are  called the generators of these  transforms and you can calculate what  the generator is for any transformation  that you come up with if I encountered  some exotic system and noticed it was  symmetric under some transformation  there is a mathematical way for me to  calculate what's conserved there's also  a converse say I noticed some mysterious  new quantity is conserved in some system  I'm interested in no this theorem says  that that conservation is from some  symmetry and the conserved quantity is  the generator of that transformation so  I can calculate what that transformation  was that's very powerful but the theorem  is amazing because it is just as  beautiful as it is useful symmetries  appeal to us and seem natural we think  it makes sense that if the  universe was shifted or rotated that  nothing should change there's no  difference between here and there so  showing that symmetry and conservation  laws are equivalent shows that  conservation laws must be just as  natural homework this time is pretty  gentle I want you to let me know what  you think of this idea have you heard of  it before maybe you've heard about  things like supersymmetry in physics try  and find out how that's related to this  the version of know--this theorem I  talked about here is the one for  classical physics only a much less  powerful version of the theorem than the  one that she actually created if you  know some calculus and classical physics  maybe try and find one of the priests  for this theorem and this is a fun  activity try and come up with strange  systems with strange symmetries and see  if you can figure out what's conserved
 

